{
  "passage": "Lakshmi's family owns a small plot of land in a village in Bihar. The plot needs only two people to do all the work of ploughing, sowing and harvesting. But five members of the family work on it, because there is no other work for them in the village. If three of them stopped working on the farm, the amount of grain produced would stay the same. These three people seem to be employed, but their work adds nothing to the output. Economists call this disguised unemployment. It is common in Indian agriculture, where many families share small pieces of land. There is another kind of unemployment in villages. Farming in many areas depends on the rain, and there is a lot of work at the time of sowing and harvesting. In the months between, there is little to do, and many workers sit idle. This is called seasonal unemployment. Lakshmi's cousin, who works on other people's farms during the harvest and then has no work for four months, faces seasonal unemployment. Unemployment wastes a country's human resource. People who could add to the national income instead become dependent on others. To create more work, the government runs schemes such as the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act, which promises 100 days of paid work in a year to rural households.",
